-- title:
--   Symmetry of the modular polynomial at squarefree level
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number which is squarefree and satisfies $N > 1$, and let `data` be a `ModularPolynomialData N`, that is: a polynomial $\Phi \in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ which is monic as a polynomial in $Y$, whose degree in $Y$ equals $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$, and which vanishes at the pair of formal Laurent series $(j(q), j(q^N))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ — precisely, evaluating $\Phi$ in $Y$ at `jqN N` with coefficients mapped by the ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}[X] \to \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ sending $X$ to `jq` gives $0$. The conclusion is the predicate `EvalSymm` for $\Phi$: for all formal Laurent series $x, y$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, the value obtained by substituting $x$ for the coefficient variable $X$ and $y$ for $Y$ equals the value obtained by substituting $y$ for $X$ and $x$ for $Y$; in classical notation, $\Phi(x, y) = \Phi(y, x)$ identically on $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$. Note that the symmetry is asserted as an identity of evaluations at Laurent-series arguments, not as an equality of bivariate polynomials.
--
--   This is the classical symmetry $\Phi_N(X,Y) = \Phi_N(Y,X)$ of the modular polynomial of level $N$, here at squarefree level $N>1$ (at level $1$ the polynomial is $Y-X$, which is antisymmetric). It is used in the study of the function fields generated by $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ and their reductions, being cited in the construction of place specialisations for fibre models in characteristic $p$ and in the computation of the relevant degrees modulo a prime.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_ModularPolynomialData_evalSymm_of_squarefree.lean

